## Settled Direction

- Extend repository-root `.tool-shed-policy.json` with one versioned `release_identity` object.
  Absence means the unchanged strict-SemVer policy. The first alternative is a bounded
  fixed-width dotted-numeric profile sufficient for `v00.05.00`; arbitrary regex and executable
  hooks remain out of scope.
- Centralize eligibility, exact identity, reachability, predecessor continuity, selection, and
  diagnostics in one release-identity evaluator consumed by every cohort operation.
- Prefer the most recent finalized cohort's recorded exact release tag when it remains eligible
  under the current project policy and reachable from the relevant commit. For bootstrap or
  unusable-history recovery, rank reachable eligible tag commits by deterministic Git topology;
  normalized aliases on one selected commit fail closed rather than silently changing identity.
- Keep the existing terminal cycle lifecycle and add an explicit administrative terminal
  disposition/projection unless implementation evidence proves this cannot remain unambiguous.
  Outcome success remains a separate verdict.
- Implement terminal cleanup as read-only planning plus exact guarded apply. The apply transaction
  rechecks every run, operation, and assignment; retires only assignments proven stale by the
  settled rule; closes the campaign; and writes one complete audit event atomically.

